WEBVTT

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:32.219
I first started watching how I met your mother when my now-husband recommended it to me about a year-end today and I've washed it over and over and I think the reason why I keep going back to it is because at some point all of the characters choose themselves, whether that's Lily going and following her during this being an artist, whether it's Robin going and being a global reporter, it's something that you see everyone do and everyone struggle with.
我第一次看《老爸老妈浪漫史》是我现在的丈夫大约一年前向我推荐的，我反复看了很多遍，我想我之所以一直重温这部剧，是因为在某个时刻，所有的角色都选择了自己，无论是莉莉去追求她作为艺术家的梦想，还是罗宾去当一名全球记者，这是你看到每个人都会做、也都会为此挣扎的事情。

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:37.719
but also something that you see their circle support them through, whether they're struggling with it or not.
但这也是你看到他们的圈子在支持他们的事情，无论他们是否在为此挣扎。

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:41.539
And I think that's just something that's really important to you as a young adult.
我认为这对年轻人来说非常重要。

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:47.500
So I've looked to the show for a camper, but also for that support to remind me, choose yourself.
所以我从这部剧中寻找慰藉，也寻找那种支持，提醒自己：选择你自己。

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.340
You deserve it.
你值得拥有。

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:56.979
I'm alone, I'll be soon in New York City when I see you.
我是一个人，我很快就会到纽约市见到你们。

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:06.180
Please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
请允许我在纽约市告诉你们一切。

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:06.680
city.
城市。

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:13.052
Well, hello and welcome to how we made your mother where I, Josh Radner, talk with Craig Thomas.
大家好，欢迎收听《老爸老妈浪漫史》，我是乔什·拉德诺，今天我和克雷格·托马斯一起聊天。

00:01:13.052 --> 00:01:13.915
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:01:13.915 --> 00:01:14.578
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:19.424
We talk about a show we made together for nine years way back in the day.
我们聊的是我们很久以前一起制作了九年的那部剧。

00:01:19.424 --> 00:01:21.814
It was called How I Met Your Mother.
它叫《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:23.141
We did 208 episodes.
我们一共拍了208集。

00:01:23.141 --> 00:01:41.528
I don't even know what episode we're on right now, but we're having a great time rewatching it from our older wiser selves, unpacking what we did, what worked, what didn't work, what what we love, why people still talk about the show today, like that lovely letter we received.
我甚至不知道我们现在聊到第几集了，但我们以更成熟、更睿智的视角重温这部剧，剖析我们当时做了什么，什么有效，什么无效，我们热爱什么，为什么人们今天还在谈论这部剧，就像我们收到的那封可爱的信一样，这感觉真的很棒。

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:42.719
How did that land on you, Craig?
克雷格，你对此有什么感想？

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:43.719
I love it.
我很喜欢。

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:45.019
I love that reading of it.
我喜欢那段解读。

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:52.200
It's such a like, that wasn't about the romance, that wasn't about the sort of the narrative style that was about characters choosing themselves.
这真的很棒，那不是关于浪漫，也不是关于叙事风格，而是关于角色们选择了自己。

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.480
That's really interesting and watching these characters have a passion and chase it.
这真的很有趣，看着这些角色拥有激情并去追逐它。

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.680
Some of us make sacrifices to chase their dream and it is, it's that time of life.
我们中的一些人为了追逐梦想做出了牺牲，这就是人生的那个阶段。

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.920
the show has that energy in it.
这部剧里有那种能量。

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:08.319
And that's the age that we were, Josh, when we were our younger, less wise selves.
乔什，那正是我们当时年轻、还没那么睿智时的年纪。

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:12.039
And we were chasing our dream in that exact moment.
我们在那一刻正追逐着我们的梦想。

00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:15.159
So it's cool that I feel like that energy is imbued into the show.
所以我觉得那种能量被注入到这部剧中，这很酷。

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:18.360
And that makes me happy to hear this young person saying that.
听到这个年轻人这么说，我感到很高兴。

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.599
That's so old when I say that.
我说这话的时候显得太老了。

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:21.599
This young person.
这个年轻人。

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:35.439
I also, I think I said this first season, I said if the show was only being watched by single people in big cities in the Northeast, It wouldn't be recognized in Brazil and India.
我还记得第一季时我说过，如果这部剧只被东北部大城市的单身人士观看，它就不会在巴西和印度受到认可。

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:36.439
I absolutely.
绝对是这样。

00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:39.840
So I just love Brazilian this for more years.
所以我很喜欢巴西人看这部剧看了这么多年。

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.000
From the accent, I don't want to assume.
从口音听，我不想妄下定论。

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.800
It didn't sound like a native New York.
听起来不像地道的纽约人。

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.280
Did we know where she was from, though?
我们知道她来自哪里吗？

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.280
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.280
We don't know.
我们不知道。

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.120
She said it.
她说了。

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:50.960
Did she say it?
她说了吗？

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.879
I mean, you said the words that we've already forgotten.
我是说，你说了我们已经忘记的那些话。

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:53.719
Oh my god.
天哪。

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:54.560
No, she didn't say where she was from.
不，她没说她来自哪里。

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:55.400
I don't think so.
我不这么认为。

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.240
No, she didn't.
不，她没说。

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.080
Okay.
好吧。

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:57.919
This is a real test.
这真是一个考验。

00:02:57.919 --> 00:02:59.199
Just rewind and find out that we're wrong.
倒回去看看我们是不是错了。

00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:00.479
This is how old we all are.
这就是我们现在有多老了。

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:05.479
But that was a great message, I love hearing that.
但那是一条很棒的信息，我喜欢听这个。

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:08.800
I love hearing that specific angle, it's not what we hear every week.
我喜欢听那个特定的角度，这不是我们每周都能听到的。

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:17.759
There's so many different ways that people find to fall in love with the show and enter into the lore of the show and why they keep coming back to watch it.
人们爱上这部剧、进入这部剧的传说，以及他们不断回来重温的原因有很多种。

00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:26.000
I mean, it's, yeah, and also you hear so much of my boyfriend introduced me to this, my wife introduced me to this, you know.
我是说，是的，而且你经常听到“我男朋友给我推荐了这个”、“我妻子给我推荐了这个”，你知道的。

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.520
Yeah, it's how people met their other.
是的，这就是人们如何遇到他们的另一半。

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:30.360
I always say a couple of times.
我总是说好几次。

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.180
A lot of times.
很多次。

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.860
So watch how I met your mother together, stay together, so.
所以一起看《老爸老妈浪漫史》，然后在一起，所以。

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.740
It seems, we go there almost every other week.
看来我们几乎每隔一周就会提到这个。

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.460
We hear a story that includes that.
我们听到的故事里都包含这个。

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.379
So yeah, it's amazing.
所以是的，这太神奇了。

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:41.099
Well, today we're deep into season three.
好了，今天我们深入探讨第三季。

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:45.860
We're on episode 11, which is called the Platinum Rule.
我们现在聊的是第11集，叫《白金法则》。

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.699
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:46.699 --> 00:03:48.939
Alec, tell us when this episode aired.
亚历克，告诉我们这一集是什么时候播出的。

00:03:48.939 --> 00:03:53.259
This aired on December 10th in the year 2007.
这一集是在2007年12月10日播出的。

00:03:53.259 --> 00:03:54.900
Okay, okay.
好的，好的。

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.199
This episode, the Platinum Rule was written by, and I hope I'm pronouncing this correctly.
这一集《白金法则》的编剧是——我希望我发音正确——

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.319
Carter Bays, and Kate Thomas.
卡特·贝斯和凯特·托马斯。

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:05.080
That's the thomas, guys.
那是托马斯，伙计们。

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.080
It is thomas.
是托马斯。

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.000
Thank you for saying that a lot of people think it's thomas.
谢谢你这么说，很多人以为是托马斯（Thomas）。

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.599
It is not, it's thomas.
不是，是托马斯（Tomas）。

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:12.000
I'm so glad I'm finally getting that out there.
我很高兴终于把这一点说清楚了。

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.319
Yeah, this was written by us.
是的，这是我们写的。

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.839
This is one of the weirdest episodes, not the episode itself.
这是最奇怪的一集之一，不是说这一集本身。

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.040
Although the episode is weird.
虽然这一集确实很奇怪。

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:21.959
And the episode is weird.
而且这一集很奇怪。

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:22.959
The episode is super weird.
这一集超级奇怪。

00:04:22.959 --> 00:04:24.720
There's like four timelines happening.
里面大概有四条时间线。

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.360
Like it's a Russian nesting dollas of like flashbacks within flashbacks.
就像俄罗斯套娃一样，闪回套着闪回。

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.399
It's great, I really like that structure.
这很棒，我真的很喜欢这种结构。

00:04:31.399 --> 00:04:37.120
It was a very strange episode in the sausage making process, and I'll explain why.
在制作过程中，这是一集非常奇怪的剧集，我会解释原因。

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:47.319
This was the exact week that the Writers Guild of America went on strike Sunday at midnight before this production week started.
这正是美国编剧工会罢工的那一周，就在这一周制作开始前的周日午夜。

00:04:47.319 --> 00:05:08.600
So what would have normally happened is we would have Monday morning, done a table read, gotten notes, heard the actors perform this, rewritten it till late at night, Monday night, put out a new script that gets to the actors late at night, the care of some wonderful PAs driving around way too late because we didn't think to email in that era, we had to drive them places.
所以通常情况下，周一早上我们会进行剧本围读，得到反馈，听演员们表演，周一晚上熬夜重写，深夜把新剧本送到演员手中，由一些很棒的制片助理开车送到各处，因为那个时代我们还没想到用电子邮件，我们必须开车送过去。

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:34.319
And then Tuesday we would have done a run through, the actors would have gotten it on their feet, they've worked on it all morning and then around lunch time or a little bit after, I just figured exactly what time run through is usually where we would see it, we would see the entire thing acted out like a play, us, the studio, the network, some of the crucial production crew, really get it in our heads what it's gonna look like and feel like go back, get notes, go back to the restroom, do another rewrite.
然后周二我们会进行排练，演员们会站起来表演，他们整个上午都在排练，然后在午餐时间或之后，我记不清具体时间了，排练通常是我们能看到整部剧像舞台剧一样演出来的时候，我们、制片厂、电视台、一些关键的制作人员，真正弄清楚它看起来和感觉起来会是什么样，然后回去，得到反馈，回去休息室，再进行一次重写。

00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:36.439
None of that happened.
这一切都没有发生。

00:05:36.439 --> 00:05:38.439
No writers were on set for it.
片场没有编剧。

00:05:38.439 --> 00:05:40.399
There were no writers anywhere.
到处都没有编剧。

00:05:40.399 --> 00:05:45.480
There was no process where we got cuts and edited it because that was considered crossing the biggest line.
没有剪辑和修改的过程，因为那被认为是越过了最大的红线。

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.240
There were no writers on set whispering all lines to you for different takes.
片场没有编剧在拍摄时为你耳语台词。

00:05:49.240 --> 00:06:00.040
Zero alts, no jokes, because that is a big deal when you're shooting a re-shot three days, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, And there was always the two writers, at least on set, the writer of the episode.
没有备选台词，没有笑话，因为当你拍摄三天的重拍戏时，这可是件大事，周三、周四、周五，而且通常至少有两个编剧在片场，这一集的编剧。

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.079
And usually then like someone that was paired with them, whether it was me or Carter in early seasons.
通常还有和他们搭档的人，无论是我还是卡特，在早期几季里。

00:06:04.079 --> 00:06:05.079
And then just...
然后只是……

00:06:05.079 --> 00:06:06.399
Chris Harris would have offered me the title.
克里斯·哈里斯本会给我这个头衔。

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:07.480
Chris Harris had a senior writer on it.
克里斯·哈里斯是资深编剧。

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.319
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:12.639
And always getting all, it's coming up with new ideas, tweaking lines, refining things.
总是会有备选方案，想出新点子，调整台词，润色内容。

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.639
None of that happened on this episode.
这一集里这些都没有发生。

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:18.879
And it is therefore the episode that sticks in my head, the least.
因此，这是我脑海中印象最浅的一集。

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:22.480
So when I watched this episode, I went, oh, Kristin Shaw was on our show.
所以当我重看这一集时，我说，哦，克里斯汀·肖竟然演过我们的剧。

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:23.480
Delightful.
真令人高兴。

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.480
I like it.
我喜欢它。

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:25.480
She's delightful.
她很讨人喜欢。

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.480
I would have liked to have spent some time with her.
我本来希望能和她多待一会儿。

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.399
I really would have liked to have met her.
我真的很希望能见到她。

00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:29.399
I did not.
但我没见到。

00:06:29.399 --> 00:06:30.399
As far as I know.
据我所知是这样。

00:06:30.399 --> 00:06:31.399
I don't think I did.
我不觉得我见过。

00:06:31.399 --> 00:06:32.399
Wait, Craig.
等等，克雷格。

00:06:32.399 --> 00:06:33.399
Help me out.
帮帮我。

00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:34.839
Remind me of this.
提醒我一下这件事。

00:06:34.839 --> 00:06:41.040
So was it like Sunday night like you realized the strike was authorized Sunday night?
所以是在周日晚上，你意识到罢工在周日晚上获得授权了吗？

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.560
Was there a chance maybe the strike will be averted?
当时有没有可能罢工会被避免？

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.560
No.
没有。

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.560
We knew it was happening.
我们知道罢工会发生。

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.720
We knew Sunday at midnight it was going to be the strike and it was down to the minute.
我们知道周日午夜罢工就要开始了，而且是精确到分钟的。

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.279
So maybe we learned on Friday or Saturday.
所以我们可能是周五或周六得知的。

00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:56.240
So you spent that weekend remembering this episode into as good a shape as you can.
所以那个周末你们都在努力把这一集修改到尽可能好的状态。

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.240
Yes, I can remember.
是的，我记得。

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.480
To am it to Pam and say you got to shoot this.
为了把它交给帕姆，告诉她你得拍这个。

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:08.600
From our being our Sunday night, with I think maybe me, Carter, and maybe Chris Harris, a writer's assistant, and just being like, let's just get this as good as it can be.
从我们周日晚上开始，我想可能是我、卡特，还有克里斯·哈里斯（一位编剧助理），我们就在想，让我们把它做到最好吧。

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.879
We're never gonna hear it, we're never gonna tweak it.
我们再也听不到它了，也再没法修改它了。

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:15.439
Writing is rewriting, except this time, there's no rewriting.
写作就是重写，但这一次，没有重写了。

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:18.319
By the time you guys were editing this, were you still on strikes?
当你们剪辑这一集的时候，你们还在罢工吗？

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:19.519
We weren't editing it.
我们没有剪辑它。

00:07:19.519 --> 00:07:21.560
We didn't edit it, we didn't edit it.
我们没剪辑，我们没剪辑。

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.279
So Sue and Pam edited this?
所以是苏和帕姆剪辑的？

00:07:23.279 --> 00:07:24.980
Sue and Pam edited it.
是苏和帕姆剪辑的。

00:07:26.079 --> 00:07:35.399
I'm trying to think if maybe one round of notes was given sort of in good faith by me and Carter, maybe that we were like, we don't feel comfortable or not supposed to.
我在想，我和卡特是否以某种诚意给过一轮笔记，也许我们当时觉得，我们不舒服或者不应该这样做。

00:07:35.399 --> 00:07:39.800
I literally don't remember, I'm not just saying that to like, to say innocent.
我真的不记得了，我不是为了装无辜才这么说的。

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.639
Did we come in for a table read on Monday for this episode?
周一我们为这一集进行剧本围读了吗？

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:43.639
I don't even remember.
我甚至都不记得了。

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:44.680
You tell me, Brown.
你告诉我吧，布朗。

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.480
I don't remember.
我不记得了。

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.759
I don't remember.
我不记得了。

00:07:47.759 --> 00:07:49.319
Three to two hundred eight episodes.
三到两百零八集。

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:50.240
I don't remember.
我不记得了。

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.079
But this is a black hole for me.
但这对我来说是个黑洞。

00:07:53.079 --> 00:07:54.839
It's so weird.
太奇怪了。

00:07:54.839 --> 00:07:56.120
I have no story for this guy.
关于这个家伙我没有任何故事。

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.720
I believe you don't remember this 19 years ago.
我相信你不记得19年前的事了。

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.879
But this thing you didn't do, 19 years like that.
但这件事你没做，19年就像那样过去了。

00:08:02.879 --> 00:08:10.360
I mean, of course, what I want you to say is like, I remember that week, Craig, we didn't, we were lost without all of you writers and on set.
我是说，当然，我希望你说的是，克雷格，我记得那个星期，我们没有，没有你们这些编剧在片场，我们都迷失了。

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.519
You're saying you're pretty good without you guys.
你是在说没有你们，你们也做得很好。

00:08:12.519 --> 00:08:18.240
You've picked up none of my hints for you to jump in and say that and it's just fucking tumble.
你完全没领会我的暗示，没顺着话说，真是冷场了。

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:29.399
But I don't remember like, I don't remember a table read where it was like us, Michael Shea, Pam, and like Alex and just some executives.
但我真的不记得了，我不记得有那种只有我们、迈克尔·谢、帕姆、亚历克斯和一些高管参加的剧本围读。

00:08:29.399 --> 00:08:30.240
You wouldn't have done it.
你们不会那样做的。

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.360
I think we didn't do a table read.
我想我们没有进行剧本围读。

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.720
You would have just started getting it on its feet.
你们可能直接就开始排练了。

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:38.360
You would have started getting it on its feet on the stage, on the sets, and walking through it.
你们可能直接在舞台上、在布景里开始排练，并走位了。

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.240
You probably did two days of that instead of one day of that.
你们可能为此做了两天，而不是一天。

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.080
And that was it.
就是这样。

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.200
You probably sat in the main set.
你们可能坐在主场景里。

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:54.279
You probably sat in Ted's living room and read it through just to read it, but not cause anyone was gonna rewrite it or change a word, not a word changed.
你们可能坐在泰德的客厅里读了一遍，只是为了读一遍，而不是因为有人要重写或改动一个字，一个字都没改。

00:08:54.279 --> 00:08:58.519
So this was the only episode we did when you guys were on strike.
所以这是你们罢工期间我们做的唯一一集。

00:08:58.519 --> 00:09:00.519
Yes, this one just goes over.
是的，这一集刚好赶上。

00:09:00.519 --> 00:09:04.960
It was just cause you had it like queued up ready to go that we were allowed to do it.
只是因为你们把它排好了准备开拍，我们才被允许做。

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.159
We had it close enough.
我们当时已经准备得差不多了。

00:09:06.159 --> 00:09:12.159
Just to explain to people, so like we, the actors are in the screen actors guild.
给观众解释一下，我们演员属于演员工会。

00:09:12.159 --> 00:09:14.600
Carter and Craig were in the writers guild, the WGA.
卡特和克雷格属于编剧工会，即WGA。

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.919
They're totally different unions.
它们是完全不同的工会。

00:09:16.919 --> 00:09:20.960
which means that the Screen Actors Guild was not on strike.
这意味着演员工会当时没有罢工。

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.639
The Directors Guild, Pam, and Michael Shea, not on strike.
导演工会、帕姆和迈克尔·谢也没有罢工。

00:09:24.639 --> 00:09:28.919
The Heron Makeup and all the crew, their unions, not on strike.
发型、化妆和所有工作人员的工会也没有罢工。

00:09:28.919 --> 00:09:31.879
The only union that was on strike was the WGA.
唯一罢工的工会是WGA。

00:09:31.879 --> 00:09:34.559
So the writers had to go home.
所以编剧们不得不回家。

00:09:34.559 --> 00:09:41.759
The writers deal was up, this was 2007, it had been many years since the last strike, which I think had been the late 80s before that.
编剧的合同到期了，那是2007年，距离上一次罢工已经过去很多年了，我想上一次是在80年代末。

00:09:41.759 --> 00:09:43.679
Alec, maybe you can double check me on that.
亚历克，也许你可以帮我核实一下。

00:09:43.679 --> 00:09:45.480
But it's been a long time.
但那已经是很久以前的事了。

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.120
I remember watching David Letterman and the late 80s and they're like, we have no writers.
我记得在80年代末看大卫·莱特曼的节目，他们说，我们没有编剧了。

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.240
And they were just like filling time with weird shit.
他们就只能用一些奇怪的东西来填补时间。

00:09:51.240 --> 00:10:00.429
And I wrote you this little ditty to sing to you in New York City.
我给你写了这首小曲子，在纽约市唱给你听。

00:10:00.429 --> 00:10:10.169
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:10.169 --> 00:10:14.169
And now, back to the show.
现在，回到节目。

00:10:14.169 --> 00:10:15.809
Do you remember what the central issue was?
你还记得当时的核心议题是什么吗？

00:10:15.809 --> 00:10:17.769
Was this DVD residuals?
是DVD的剩余收益吗？

00:10:17.769 --> 00:10:20.129
Was this streaming that started to be an issue?
是当时开始成为问题的流媒体吗？

00:10:20.129 --> 00:10:23.370
Yeah, so 2007, just not streaming.
是的，2007年，还不是流媒体。

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.169
because it was just starting.
因为那时才刚刚开始。

00:10:26.169 --> 00:10:30.009
But yeah, it was the beginning, it was the seeds of all of that.
但确实，那是开始，是所有这一切的种子。

00:10:30.009 --> 00:10:35.009
Of, all right, our written content is creeping off of CBS.
关于，好吧，我们编写的内容正在从哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）流失。

00:10:36.289 --> 00:10:38.730
It's creeping off of network television and cable television.
它正在从电视网和有线电视中流失。

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:39.850
It's creeping onto DVDs.
它正在流向DVD。

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.289
More DVDs are still a very big deal then.
当时DVD仍然是非常重要的东西。

00:10:42.289 --> 00:10:44.330
And writers were getting peanuts of that.
而编剧们从中得到的报酬微乎其微。

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.730
And studios were making a lot of money on that.
但制片厂却从中赚了很多钱。

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:51.730
The content was starting to be in places like you could buy the show on iTunes and watch it.
内容开始出现在一些地方，比如你可以在iTunes上购买节目并观看。

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.929
And everyone new streaming was coming.
每个人都知道流媒体时代即将到来。

00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:57.529
I don't know exactly where it would have been at this moment in 2007.
我不知道2007年这一刻具体发展到了什么程度。

00:10:57.529 --> 00:10:59.450
I'm not, maybe you can double check me on that too.
我不确定，也许你也可以帮我核实一下。

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:06.009
I like, I don't think there was their digital like Netflix that wasn't just like DVDs and pouches in 2007.
我不觉得当时有像Netflix那样的数字平台，2007年除了DVD和光盘袋之外什么都没有。

00:11:06.009 --> 00:11:06.850
I don't think so.
我不觉得有。

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:08.970
So a year or two before that started, right?
所以是在那之后一两年才开始的，对吧？

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:10.370
Do you know what's a crazy thing?
你知道最疯狂的是什么吗？

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:15.490
Like basically all these strikes have to happen when new technology comes on.
基本上所有这些罢工都必须在新技术出现时发生。

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.889
Yes, so it was that theme.
是的，就是那个主题。

00:11:17.889 --> 00:11:18.730
It was that theme.
就是那个主题。

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.990
It was a very early moment of that theme.
那是那个主题非常早期的时刻。

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:33.549
And the big question was the same, which is, wait a second, there's all these rules for our written work, your active work, Pam's directed work, to be used in this main way it's used.
而核心问题是一样的，那就是，等一下，我们编写的作品、你们表演的作品、帕姆导演的作品，在主要的传播方式下都有各种规则。

00:11:33.549 --> 00:11:36.429
Now that's changing, you're gonna use it all these other ways.
现在情况变了，你们要用所有这些其他方式来使用它。

00:11:36.429 --> 00:11:38.389
Pay us very, very little for that.
却只付给我们非常、非常少的钱。

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:42.750
And you guys, the studios and the big companies are still gonna make a lot of money on it.
而你们这些制片厂和大公司却依然能从中赚取巨额利润。

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:51.830
Hey, we have a problem, we're the ones creating this material that you're gonna make a lot of money on and we have no fair equation for compensation for that.
嘿，我们遇到了一个问题，我们是这些内容的创作者，你们靠这些内容赚了大钱，但我们却没有获得公平的报酬。

00:11:53.669 --> 00:11:55.230
And it's really exactly the same.
情况确实完全一样。

00:11:55.230 --> 00:12:16.429
There has been another writer's guild strike since then a couple of years ago that is very much about all of these issues, but now through the lens of streaming and the changing of the industry and the shrinking of the industry, the shrinking of writer's rooms, the shrinking of having writers on set and in production and all these other issues that are related to the issues in 2007.
自那以后，几年间又发生了一次编剧工会罢工，主要也是围绕这些问题，但现在的背景是流媒体的兴起、行业的变革与萎缩、编剧室的缩减、片场编剧人数的减少，以及所有与2007年罢工相关的问题。

00:12:16.429 --> 00:12:20.149
And so what happened was we knew it was coming.
所以当时的情况是，我们预感到罢工要来了。

00:12:20.149 --> 00:12:21.710
We were hoping it would be averted.
我们曾希望能够避免。

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:26.710
It wasn't, we hit send around 11.59 on Sunday night.
但没能避免，我们在周日晚上11点59分左右发出了罢工通知。

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.309
And we said, I hope this goes well.
我们当时说，希望一切顺利。

00:12:30.309 --> 00:12:34.070
And then we started picketing around your own studio.
然后我们就开始在你们的制片厂外进行纠察。

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.509
The studio lot while you shot the episode.
就在你们拍摄那一集的时候，在制片厂外。

00:12:36.509 --> 00:12:38.230
I think it's during lunch.
我想是在午餐时间。

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:39.629
We would come out and pick it with you.
我们会出来和你们一起纠察。

00:12:39.629 --> 00:12:42.470
Like, I know we joined the picket line a little bit.
比如，我知道我们加入纠察线有一会儿了。

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:43.590
You would join us.
你们会加入我们。

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:48.909
I was most, the funny thing is, because my son was born right between your two and three.
最有趣的是，因为我的儿子正好出生在你们第二季和第三季之间。

00:12:48.909 --> 00:12:55.590
And so I was, for me, and it was many months, it was like, maybe you can double check me on this too, Alec, you're my fact-decker today.
对我来说，那持续了好几个月，也许你可以帮我核实一下，亚历克，你今天就是我的事实核查员。

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:58.230
I think that strike was about three and a half months.
我想那次罢工持续了大约三个半月。

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.389
I think it was something like that.
我想大概是那样。

00:12:59.389 --> 00:13:12.230
I could be wrong, but it was three months, I actually got all this time with my six month old boy, I wouldn't have, and my wife who was exhausted by that point was like, you know what you can do when you're picketing?
我可能记错了，但确实有三个月，我实际上得到了和六个月大的儿子相处的宝贵时间，否则我根本没机会，我当时已经精疲力竭的妻子说，你知道你在纠察的时候能做什么吗？

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:13.610
push a stroller.
推婴儿车。

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:17.230
So I would go to the Elliott walk the picket line with you.
所以我带着埃利奥特去和你一起走纠察线。

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.669
Oh yeah, all of it's a lot.
哦，是的，这一切都很不容易。

00:13:18.669 --> 00:13:23.590
And I would go, I lived walking distance in Culver City from the Sony lot.
我会去，我住在卡尔弗城，离索尼制片厂很近，走路就能到。

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:25.269
And I could see the Sony lot.
我能看到索尼制片厂。

00:13:25.269 --> 00:13:28.870
If I walked out my front door and just peek my head, I could see the Sony lot two blocks away.
如果我走出前门，探出头，就能看到两个街区外的索尼制片厂。

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:35.429
So I'd walk over there with Elliott and just push him around, maybe had a sign and it was sort of just like fishing around and pick it.
所以我带着埃利奥特走过去，推着他转悠，也许手里拿个牌子，就像是在纠察线上闲逛。

00:13:35.429 --> 00:13:40.269
But a lot of the rest of the Hemium writers mostly went to where we were.
但其他大部分《老爸老妈浪漫史》的编剧大多去了我们所在的地方。

00:13:40.269 --> 00:13:42.669
Elliot has like a weird taste for social justice.
埃利奥特对社会正义有一种奇怪的品味。

00:13:42.669 --> 00:13:43.669
He doesn't know why.
他自己都不知道为什么。

00:13:43.669 --> 00:13:44.669
I think he does.
我想他知道。

00:13:44.669 --> 00:13:45.669
Yeah.
是的。

00:13:45.669 --> 00:13:46.669
He's just like in his bloodstream.
这就像在他的血液里一样。

00:13:46.669 --> 00:13:48.629
Let me just throw out the fact here.
让我在这里抛出一个事实。

00:13:48.629 --> 00:13:54.429
So yeah, the one before that was 1988 and that went for 22 weeks.
在那之前的一次是1988年，持续了22周。

00:13:54.429 --> 00:14:00.549
And yeah, they're saying that it was about cable TV and VHS and it was the new technology of the day.
当时主要是关于有线电视和录像带，那是当时的新技术。

00:14:00.549 --> 00:14:05.149
And this one I see is being listed as having lasted in 2007 for a hundred days.
而这次我看到记录显示2007年的罢工持续了一百天。

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:06.750
So yeah, just over three months also.
所以，确实也是三个多月。

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.750
Wow.
哇。

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:08.750
It was long.
那段日子很漫长。

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:20.429
And it was so weird, so there was a huge gap between this episode and the next episode, which is called No Tomorrow of several months, between those two airing.
而且非常奇怪，这一集和下一集（名为《没有明天》）之间有几个月的巨大空档。

00:14:20.429 --> 00:14:27.629
And I remember it just being so scary because our show was getting a lot of momentum in year three.
我记得当时非常害怕，因为我们的剧在第三年势头正猛。

00:14:27.629 --> 00:14:29.909
We weren't a big hit yet, but we were in year three.
我们还算不上大热剧，但我们正处于第三年。

00:14:29.909 --> 00:14:30.909
We'd found our footing.
我们已经站稳了脚跟。

00:14:30.909 --> 00:14:32.350
I felt, I mean, I don't know how you feel, Josh.
我的意思是，我不知道你怎么想，乔什。

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:36.870
I feel like creatively to this point, we've just done this rewatch, we're 11 episodes in.
我觉得从创作角度来看，我们刚刚完成了重温，已经看了11集了。

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:37.870
I feel like the season's great.
我觉得这一季很棒。

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.950
I don't think things are going really well.
我不觉得事情进展得很顺利。

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.629
And I was fucking terrified.
我当时真是吓坏了。

00:14:43.629 --> 00:14:45.590
We're hitting this brick wall.
我们撞上了一堵墙。

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:53.669
We're fighting against the corporate overlords who we're asking for money, millions of dollars to make this show and to keep our show on the air.
我们在对抗那些企业大佬，我们要求他们投入数百万美元来制作这部剧，让它继续播出。

00:14:53.669 --> 00:14:58.750
What if during this strike, if it goes on, non-non, shows start getting canceled and cut?
如果罢工持续下去，万一剧集被取消或砍掉怎么办？

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:01.690
And it's like, okay, you guys want more money and you're robbing us of product?
他们可能会想，好吧，你们想要更多的钱，还要断我们的财路？

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:05.269
Fuck you, we're gonna cancel these 12 shows and we're one of them.
去你的，我们要取消这12部剧，而我们就是其中之一。

00:15:05.269 --> 00:15:07.389
It was a very scary time.
那是一段非常可怕的时期。

00:15:07.389 --> 00:15:10.789
You know, so much has to go right for a show to run nine years.
你知道，一部剧要播九年，必须有太多事情进展顺利。

00:15:10.789 --> 00:15:12.750
Like so much has to go right.
真的，太多事情必须顺遂。

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:13.669
How many bullets do you have to die?
你要躲过多少颗子弹？

00:15:13.669 --> 00:15:23.990
You don't want to remind me of like, you look at a career like LeBron James or Michael Jordan, like they're, they had that career, but for they, they didn't get injured.
你不想提醒我，看看勒布朗·詹姆斯或迈克尔·乔丹的职业生涯，他们之所以有那样的成就，是因为他们没有受伤。

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:26.870
Like they didn't have, they didn't come down on their ankle.
他们没有遭遇那种情况。

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.750
One play could have changed those historic careers, you know?
一次比赛就可能改变那些历史性的职业生涯，你知道吗？

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:31.590
Yeah.
是的。

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:43.549
I wanted to say one thing about these strikes that come up, and I hope this isn't two in the weeds for people, you know, They just, you know, because this really is about how the show was made and what was going on.
关于这些罢工，我想说一件事，希望这不会让大家觉得太枯燥，因为这确实关乎这部剧是如何制作的以及当时发生了什么。

00:15:43.549 --> 00:15:51.629
And this is like, this is actually like a really big story in terms of this episode and how it was made and all this stuff.
这实际上是关于这一集及其制作过程的一个非常重要的故事。

00:15:51.629 --> 00:15:52.750
So I'm glad we're talking about this.
所以我很高兴我们能谈论这个。

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:53.750
I hope this is interesting people.
希望大家觉得有趣。

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.509
It's very interesting to me.
对我来说非常有趣。

00:15:55.509 --> 00:16:00.750
But my brother-in-law Gideon told me, we were having a talk about AI.
但我姐夫吉迪恩告诉我，我们当时在讨论人工智能。

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:11.950
And he said, and I think this is historically true, that any time there's a new communication medium, it introduced, millions of people die.
他说，我认为这在历史上是真实的，每当出现一种新的传播媒介，就会导致数百万人死亡。

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:23.190
Like there's millions of like wars and displacement, like the printing press, like there were massive wars in Asia, massive, you know, disruptions throughout Europe.
比如战争和流离失所，就像印刷术出现时，亚洲发生了大规模战争，欧洲各地也出现了大规模动荡。

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:32.950
Like these new technologies are not, they bring a host of anxiety to humanity, you know?
这些新技术并非没有代价，它们给人类带来了巨大的焦虑，你知道吗？

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:42.629
And so I think like when people were taking to the picket line, Like, it's really hard for people to understand how emotional making stuff is, you know?
