You can either drop us a voice note or a written note.
你可以给我们发送语音留言或文字留言。

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:20.880
We love to hear from you.
我们很乐意听到你的声音。

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:22.079
We open our show with a voice note.
我们通常以语音留言开始节目。

00:49:22.079 --> 00:49:23.767
close it with a written note.
以文字留言结束节目。

00:49:23.767 --> 00:49:25.979
Today we are actually closing the two.
今天我们实际上要用两个留言来收尾。

00:49:25.979 --> 00:49:30.286
We got two for the price of one, Alec insisted that both of these be read.
我们买一送二，Alec坚持要读这两封信。

00:49:30.286 --> 00:49:31.858
We have not read these yet.
我们还没读过这些。

00:49:31.858 --> 00:49:34.128
Craig and I, but we're very excited to.
Craig和我，但我们非常期待。

00:49:34.128 --> 00:49:36.223
Craig's going to read the first one.
Craig来读第一封。

00:49:36.223 --> 00:49:40.239
I'm going to read the second one and let's hear what you have to say.
我来读第二封，让我们听听你们想说什么。

00:49:40.239 --> 00:49:41.073
Let's do it.
开始吧。

00:49:41.073 --> 00:49:44.340
This is this letter is from Tilda from Denmark.
这封信来自丹麦的Tilda。

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:48.440
Thank you for the pronunciation of that tilde from Denmark.
谢谢你对丹麦Tilda这个名字的发音。

00:49:48.440 --> 00:49:51.400
It says, hi, Craig, Josh and Alec.
信上说：嗨，Craig、Josh和Alec。

00:49:51.400 --> 00:49:52.400
Alec, shout out.
Alec，向你致敬。

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:53.239
Hi.
嗨。

00:49:53.239 --> 00:49:54.800
And everyone else behind the scenes.
还有幕后的每一位。

00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:57.239
First of all, thank you for making this podcast.
首先，感谢你们制作了这个播客。

00:49:57.239 --> 00:50:01.920
I've always been the geeky person that watched bonus material on the DVDs back when it was a thing.
我一直是个极客，在DVD时代就喜欢看花絮。

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:04.320
Alec made that material, by the way, for how much a mother.
顺便说一下，Alec就是为《老爸老妈浪漫史》制作那些花絮的人。

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:05.159
Well, thank you.
嗯，谢谢。

00:50:05.159 --> 00:50:06.159
You're welcome.
不客气。

00:50:06.159 --> 00:50:10.159
This rewatch podcast is sort of the modern version of the DVD bonus material.
这个重温播客算是DVD花絮的现代版。

00:50:10.159 --> 00:50:14.039
Well, it's appropriate because Alec doing this too.
嗯，这很合适，因为Alec也在做这个。

00:50:14.039 --> 00:50:18.320
I love to learn about film making the process and the decisions that were made along the way.
我喜欢了解电影制作的过程以及一路走来所做的决定。

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:26.019
That interest led me to getting a master's degree in dramaturgy, back then hoping to find a job in writing or producing a film or a theater.
这种兴趣促使我获得了戏剧学硕士学位，当时希望能找到一份电影或戏剧写作或制作的工作。

00:50:26.019 --> 00:50:35.380
I fell in love with him because of the fun and often surprising ways to tell the stories, like in the Pineapple incident, Ted Mosby architect, and one of my favorites, the platinum rule.
我爱上这部剧是因为它讲述故事的方式既有趣又常常出人意料，比如《菠萝事件》、《泰德·莫斯比：建筑师》，还有我最喜欢的《白金法则》。

00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:44.940
I wrote my thesis about the unreliable narrator, and though him wasn't my subject of analysis, I think it's still influenced my decision and interest in this specific storytelling technique.
我的论文写的是“不可靠的叙述者”，虽然《老爸老妈浪漫史》不是我的分析对象，但我认为它仍然影响了我对这种特定叙事技巧的决定和兴趣。

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:48.920
I also want to let you know what Hemium means to me right now in these days.
我还想让你们知道《老爸老妈浪漫史》在这些日子里对我意味着什么。

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:51.539
My husband and I have a child with special needs.
我和丈夫有一个有特殊需求的孩子。

00:50:51.539 --> 00:50:57.260
We love him and we try to navigate the struggles of the life we're now living, but when the day is over, we are done.
我们爱他，努力应对现在生活中的挣扎，但当一天结束时，我们已经精疲力竭了。

00:50:57.260 --> 00:50:59.500
There's so little relational energy left.
剩下的情感能量太少了。

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:02.300
God, that's a good phrase for this.
天哪，这真是个贴切的说法。

00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:08.860
So little relational energy left that I don't have any left to engage with new people and not even fictional ones.
剩下的情感能量少到我没有力气去接触新的人，甚至是虚构的人物。

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:12.179
most nights I just re-watch an episode or two of Hemium.
大多数晚上，我只是重温一两集《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:51:12.179 --> 00:51:17.099
That way I get to hang out with my old pals Ted, Marshall, Lily, Robin, and Barney.
这样我就能和我的老朋友泰德、马歇尔、莉莉、罗宾和巴尼待在一起。

00:51:17.099 --> 00:51:26.998
They ask nothing of me other than 20 minutes of attention and in return they make me laugh, cry, and root for them while they remind me of hope and love and endurance.
他们不需要我做什么，只需要我花20分钟关注他们，作为回报，他们让我欢笑、流泪，并为他们加油，同时提醒我关于希望、爱和坚持。

00:51:26.998 --> 00:51:33.637
I don't know if I believe the premise of everything happens for a reason or that the universe has some big plan.
我不知道自己是否相信“万事皆有因”或者宇宙有宏大计划的前提。

00:51:33.637 --> 00:51:35.771
I have yet to see it in my own life.
我在自己的生活中还没看到过。

00:51:35.771 --> 00:51:39.091
But I like the Ted believes, and that it fuels his hope.
但我喜欢泰德的信念，它支撑着他的希望。

00:51:39.091 --> 00:51:42.469
My own hope is kind of frayed and tattered, to be honest.
老实说，我自己的希望已经有点破损和残缺了。

00:51:43.110 --> 00:51:52.784
Life seems so far from how I thought it would be, both family and work, but I do try not to let the shadow of what didn't come, dim the light of what actually is.
生活似乎与我预想的相去甚远，无论是家庭还是工作，但我确实努力不让“未实现之事”的阴影，遮蔽了“当下现实”的光芒。

00:51:52.784 --> 00:51:56.068
That is something both Hemium and Hoemium remind me of.
这是《老爸老妈浪漫史》和你们的播客提醒我的事情。

00:51:56.068 --> 00:52:02.040
And someday, when there's space in me for new fictional people, I want to check out your book, Greg.
总有一天，当我有空间容纳新的虚构人物时，我想看看你的书，Craig。

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:07.175
I would love to read a comedy about a special needs family, especially from your hand.
我很乐意读一本关于特殊需求家庭的喜剧，尤其是出自你之手的作品。

00:52:07.175 --> 00:52:09.624
It'll be no short of legendary, I expect.
我期待它会是传奇般的。

00:52:09.624 --> 00:52:12.789
Thanks for being my pals on my screen and in my ears.
感谢你们成为我屏幕上和耳朵里的伙伴。

00:52:12.789 --> 00:52:13.983
Lots of love, Tilda.
爱你们的，Tilda。

00:52:13.983 --> 00:52:16.133
Wow, I'm very moved by that message.
哇，我被那条信息深深打动了。

00:52:16.133 --> 00:52:24.971
I hope you do pick up the book, but I also understand how full your brain and your heart and your soul and every minute and every molecule is Tilda.
我希望你真的能去读那本书，但我完全理解你的大脑、心灵、灵魂以及每一分钟、每一个分子都已经满负荷了，Tilda。

00:52:24.971 --> 00:52:26.942
I'm sending you so much strength.
我为你送上满满的力量。

00:52:26.942 --> 00:52:29.509
And I'm so happy the show is there for you.
我很高兴这部剧能陪伴你。

00:52:29.829 --> 00:52:32.599
Yeah, that was really beautiful.
是的，那真的很美。

00:52:32.599 --> 00:52:34.158
Thank you so much.
非常感谢。

00:52:34.158 --> 00:52:36.408
Here is our second letter.
这是我们的第二封信。

00:52:36.408 --> 00:52:39.178
This is from Kateri from Denver.
来自丹佛的Kateri。

00:52:39.178 --> 00:52:45.324
Dear Alec, Craig, and Josh, it is nice, Alec, that people write to you.
亲爱的Alec、Craig和Josh，Alec，人们给你写信真是太好了。

00:52:45.324 --> 00:52:46.276
It is nice.
确实很好。

00:52:46.276 --> 00:52:47.142
It's nice.
很好。

00:52:47.142 --> 00:52:51.730
I'm embarrassed by my name being first in that email.
我对我的名字出现在邮件的第一位感到尴尬。

00:52:51.730 --> 00:52:53.970
You should alphabetize them.
你应该按字母顺序排列它们。

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:55.650
This is alphabetized.
这是按字母顺序排列的。

00:52:55.650 --> 00:52:56.150
Okay.
好吧。

00:52:56.150 --> 00:53:06.710
This is Dear Alec, Craig, and Josh, my name is Kateri and I am a long-term Hemium fan, a psychology professor, a wife, and a mother.
这是：亲爱的Alec、Craig和Josh，我叫Kateri，是一名长期的《老爸老妈浪漫史》粉丝、心理学教授、妻子和母亲。

00:53:06.610 --> 00:53:20.690
I'm writing to make my case that the platinum rule is the best encapsulation of the series and that it is also a science-backed example of how art can overcome the human flaws of storytelling.
我写信是为了证明《白金法则》是该系列最好的缩影，它也是一个有科学依据的例子，证明艺术如何克服人类叙事中的缺陷。

00:53:20.690 --> 00:53:24.769
Now, remember, this is a psychology professor, so I'm very excited to be here.
记住，这是一位心理学教授，所以我很兴奋能在这里。

00:53:24.769 --> 00:53:28.050
I'm terrified that this is going to be a case for writers not being muted.
我很担心这会变成作家们不被重视的案例。

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:28.550
Oh, exactly.
哦，确实。

00:53:28.550 --> 00:53:29.929
I don't know what's going to happen.
我不知道会发生什么。

00:53:29.889 --> 00:53:31.489
I haven't imagined my seat here.
我还没想象过我坐在这里的样子。

00:53:31.489 --> 00:53:41.305
The central lesson of the platinum rule is that there are some things that cannot be learned by verbal recounting alone, that one must experience instead.
《白金法则》的核心教训是，有些事情是无法仅通过口头叙述来学习的，必须亲身经历。

00:53:41.305 --> 00:53:44.428
This is supported by psychology and neuroscience.
这一点得到了心理学和神经科学的支持。

00:53:44.428 --> 00:53:52.268
Learning that is viscerally experienced is more robust than learning that is transmitted with language, storytelling alone.
通过亲身体验获得的学习，比仅通过语言和讲故事传达的学习要稳固得多。

00:53:52.268 --> 00:53:58.770
The nested structure is uniquely hymnium, quick cuts, layered flashbacks, wig work, all of it on work.
这种嵌套结构是《老爸老妈浪漫史》独有的，快速剪辑、分层闪回、假发造型，所有这些都很棒。

00:53:58.769 --> 00:54:02.050
Big props to the wig work in this episode, by the way.
顺便说一下，这一集的假发造型值得大加赞赏。

00:54:02.050 --> 00:54:08.050
All of it on point in creating a fugue-like satisfying balance of predictability and build.
所有这些都精准地创造了一种类似赋格曲的、令人满意的可预测性与构建之间的平衡。

00:54:08.050 --> 00:54:12.369
The irony of the episode contains the broader message of the entire series.
这一集的讽刺包含了整个系列更广泛的信息。

00:54:12.369 --> 00:54:17.409
Narrator Ted tries to teach this to them by telling them a story.
叙述者泰德试图通过讲故事来教导他们。

00:54:17.409 --> 00:54:26.530
Even older Ted still believes if his story is told well enough, it will spare his kids the sting of experience and convey the lesson in a safe contained way.
即使是更年长的泰德仍然相信，如果他的故事讲得足够好，就能让他的孩子们免受经历的痛苦，并以一种安全、受控的方式传达教训。

00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:29.929
The rest of the episode tells us he would fail.
这一集的其余部分告诉我们他会失败。

00:54:29.929 --> 00:54:33.170
Wow, I mean, it's a great point.
哇，我的意思是，这是一个很好的观点。

00:54:33.170 --> 00:54:34.210
It is a great point.
确实是一个很好的观点。

00:54:34.210 --> 00:54:45.010
However, where Ted as a storyteller fails, the show as an art form sores because a TV show is able to partially hack the platinum rule.
然而，泰德作为讲故事的人失败了，但作为一种艺术形式的剧集却腾飞了，因为电视剧能够部分破解“白金法则”。

00:54:45.010 --> 00:54:52.050
Research shows that between learning from experience and storytelling is a middle ground, observational learning.
研究表明，在从经验中学习和从讲故事中学习之间，存在一个中间地带：观察学习。

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:58.809
We learn better from watching, observing other people learn than we do from just hearing a story about it.
我们通过观察别人学习，比仅仅听关于它的故事学得更好。

00:54:58.809 --> 00:55:04.369
Observational learning is not quite as robust as experiential learning, but it can be quite close.
观察学习虽然不如体验式学习那么稳固，但可以非常接近。

00:55:04.369 --> 00:55:13.090
So by inviting viewers to see themselves as the sixth friend in the McLaren's booth, the show is allowing millions of people to learn vicariously.
因此，通过邀请观众将自己视为麦克拉伦酒吧卡座里的第六个朋友，该剧让数百万人能够通过代理学习。

00:55:13.090 --> 00:55:18.769
Observational learning contains just enough sting to learn, but in a removed, safer way.
观察学习包含了足够的痛苦来让人学习，但以一种疏离、更安全的方式。

00:55:18.769 --> 00:55:23.929
Hemium viewers are learning older Ted's lesson even better than Luke and Penny will.
Hemium的观众比卢克和佩妮更好地领悟了老泰德的教训。

00:55:23.929 --> 00:55:38.130
I suspect the reason your show resonates with so many people is that the audience observes and indirectly experiences that a path of winding heartbreak can lead to joy, not just a story told, but a story showed.
我猜你们的节目之所以能引起这么多人的共鸣，是因为观众观察并间接体验到，充满心碎的曲折道路最终会通向快乐，这不仅是一个被讲述的故事，更是一个被展示的故事。

00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:47.929
So thanks for creating an episode about experiential learning that uses observational learning to demonstrate that verbal learning has limited effectiveness.
所以感谢你们创作了一集关于体验式学习的剧集，它利用观察式学习证明了口头学习的效果是有限的。

00:55:47.929 --> 00:55:52.050
Thanks for your thoughtful revisiting and vulnerability on the podcast.
感谢你们在播客中进行的深刻回顾和展现出的脆弱感。

00:55:52.050 --> 00:55:57.889
It has reignited and expanded my intellectual and emotional affection for the show.
它重新点燃并扩展了我对这部剧的理智和情感上的喜爱。

00:55:57.889 --> 00:55:59.130
Kateri Kates.
卡特里·凯茨。

00:55:59.130 --> 00:55:59.969
Oh my God.
天哪。

00:55:59.969 --> 00:56:00.809
Wow.
哇。

00:56:00.809 --> 00:56:04.369
That letter, this letter is a Russian nesting now.
那封信，这封信简直是俄罗斯套娃。

00:56:04.369 --> 00:56:05.210
I know.
我知道。

00:56:05.210 --> 00:56:06.130
It's incredible.
太不可思议了。

00:56:06.130 --> 00:56:06.969
Yeah.
是啊。

00:56:06.969 --> 00:56:07.809
Well done.
做得好。

00:56:07.809 --> 00:56:08.809
Kateri.
卡特里。

00:56:08.809 --> 00:56:09.650
Do you know what's fun?
你知道什么很有趣吗？

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:10.489
My God.
天哪。

00:56:10.489 --> 00:56:15.280
I'm directing a movie hopefully next year that I didn't write.
我希望明年能执导一部电影，那不是我写的。

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:18.139
That's my first time directing a movie I didn't write.
这是我第一次执导一部非我所写的电影。

00:56:18.139 --> 00:56:36.920
And I had such a good meeting with the writer, and he told me he texted his wife after the meeting and said, I think this guy understands my movie better than I understand my movie, which was great and lovely, but I could feel that in her letter is stuff that is inarguably true.
我和编剧开了一个非常好的会，他告诉我，会后他给妻子发短信说，我觉得这家伙比我更了解我的电影，这很棒也很可爱，但我能感觉到，在她的信里，有些东西是无可辩驳的真理。

00:56:36.920 --> 00:56:39.900
I think everything she said in that is inarguably true.
我觉得她在信中所说的一切都是无可辩驳的真理。

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:46.500
but it's not something that you, if you were interviewed about the platinum rule, like you would not be with it.
但如果你被问及“白金法则”时，你可能不会想到这些。

00:56:46.500 --> 00:56:49.079
How about we just talk for 45 minutes less than 10 minutes.
我们聊了45分钟，不到10分钟。

00:56:49.079 --> 00:56:49.920
It didn't come close.
根本没提到那个。

00:56:49.920 --> 00:56:52.239
It didn't come close to that.
根本没接近那个话题。

00:56:52.239 --> 00:56:53.840
It's genius, Kateria.
这真是天才，卡特里。

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:54.679
Yeah.
是啊。

00:56:55.800 --> 00:56:58.920
But I'm saying like, she has insight into what?
但我的意思是，她对什么有洞察力？

00:56:58.920 --> 00:57:01.119
I mean, she has insights into what you guys were doing.
我是说，她对你们当时在做的事情有洞察力。

00:57:01.119 --> 00:57:01.960
Yes.
是的。

00:57:01.960 --> 00:57:05.079
And the stuff that she's pointing out is in there.
她指出的那些东西确实在里面。

00:57:05.079 --> 00:57:06.320
It's not like, it's in there.
并不是说，它就在那儿。

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:07.880
